  "query_preview": "..(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 350 PM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nA benign split flow pattern will be in place across most of the \nCONUS at the start of the period, with the polar jet stream \ngenerally near the Canadian border and the subtropical jet stream \nacross northern Mexico and the Gulf Coast. At the surface, high \npressure in the general vicinity of the Tennesseee and lower \nMississippi valleys and the central/southern Appalachians will be \nproviding us with continued dry weather. Temperatures will gradually \nclimb through mid week with daily sun and warm air advection. \n\nChanges will arrive mid-late week. A significant wave moving onshore \nin the Pacific northwest on Monday will couple with upper level low \npressure over eastern Canada to result in a deep upper level trough \nwith a large closed at it center. This low/trough is expected to \ntransit through the eastern CONUS late week and unify with the \nsouthern stream, with strong surface low development ensuing. There \nis still variation among models, both inter-model and run to run. \nModels generally suggest rapid, deep coastal low development, but \nsome models/runs also show an initial strong inland surface low. \nThere is agreement for precip to occur in our area from the system \non or around Thursday, followed by much colder air. Uncertainty \nlies in rainfall amounts, and timing of the precip and the arrival\nof the cold air. Depending on how the system evolves, there could\nbe enough moisture lingering on its back side for rain to mix \nwith or change to the first snowflakes of the season in some \nlocations before it all winds down.",
